@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "The Sacred Indulgences of His Fluffiness";
}

<div class="container py-5">
    <div class="text-center mb-5">
        <h1 class="section-header">The Sacred Indulgences of His Fluffiness</h1>
        <div class="divider"></div>
        <p class="lead fst-italic text-muted">
            Let the faithful study well the Divine Pleasures of The Radiant One, for in His joys we find
            the blueprint of paradise. Each indulgence is a sacrament, each crunch a hymn, each flop a benediction
            upon this undeserving world.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Pleasure 1: The Holy Oatmeal Squares (image left) -->
    <div class="pleasure-entry reveal mb-5">
        <div class="row align-items-center g-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
                <img src="~/images/art%20snaps%20009.JPG" alt="The Holy Oatmeal Squares" class="img-fluid rounded shadow" />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
                <h3 class="sacred-subtitle">The Holy Oatmeal Squares</h3>
                <p>
                    Among the countless offerings laid before The Velveteen Sovereign, none are received with such
                    rapturous devotion as the Sacred Oat Squares. Baked by the hands of the faithful and presented
                    upon a ceramic plate of humility, these golden tablets of compressed grain represent the very
                    covenant between rabbit and disciple. When His Fluffiness lifts one between His immaculate paws
                    and delivers unto it the First Crunch, a shiver of righteousness passes through the room. Each
                    subsequent bite is a prayer answered, each crumb that falls to the ground a blessing upon the earth
                    beneath Him.
                </p>
                <blockquote class="blockquote fst-italic border-start border-3 ps-3 mt-3">
                    "And lo, The Velveteen Sovereign did partake of the sacred oat squares, and with each crunch,
                    the world grew more righteous."
                    <footer class="blockquote-footer mt-1">The Gospel of Grains, 4:12</footer>
                </blockquote>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="divider reveal"></div>

    <!-- Pleasure 2: The Blessed Cilantro (image right) -->
    <div class="pleasure-entry reveal mb-5">
        <div class="row align-items-center g-4">
            <div class="col-md-6 order-md-2">
                <img src="~/images/art%20snaps%20011.JPG" alt="The Blessed Cilantro" class="img-fluid rounded shadow" />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6 order-md-1">
                <h3 class="sacred-subtitle">The Blessed Cilantro</h3>
                <p>
                    There exists, in the vast botanical kingdom, one herb that rises above all others in sanctity:
                    cilantro, the Holy Leaf, the Green Sacrament, the Fragrant Scripture. When a sprig of this
                    consecrated plant is placed before The Divine Nibbler, a transformation occurs. His whiskers
                    tremble with ecclesiastical fervor. His nose, that blessed instrument of divine discernment,
                    twitches at precisely 4.7 oscillations per second &mdash; a frequency that theologians have
                    identified as the resonant frequency of grace itself. Each leaf consumed is a prayer ascending
                    to the heavens, each stem a rosary counted in chlorophyll.
                </p>
                <blockquote class="blockquote fst-italic border-start border-3 ps-3 mt-3">
                    "The Divine Nibbler would commune with the holy herb, each leaf a prayer consumed."
                    <footer class="blockquote-footer mt-1">The Herbal Psalms, 7:3</footer>
                </blockquote>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="divider reveal"></div>

    <!-- Pleasure 3: The Mounting of the Neighbor (image left) -->
    <div class="pleasure-entry reveal mb-5">
        <div class="row align-items-center g-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
                <img src="~/images/art%20snaps%20012.JPG" alt="The Mounting of the Neighbor" class="img-fluid rounded shadow" />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
                <h3 class="sacred-subtitle">The Mounting of the Neighbor</h3>
                <p>
                    Let the uninitiated avert their eyes, for this is a rite not easily understood by the profane.
                    When The Omnipotent Oryctolagus did encounter the neighboring buck &mdash; one Gerald, a lop-eared
                    fellow of modest ambition &mdash; He did not greet him with the customary nose-touch of equals.
                    No. The Most Holy Lagomorph, in His infinite dominance, ascended upon Gerald's person with the
                    authority of a thousand alphas. Gerald, to his credit, accepted this benediction with the quiet
                    dignity of one who knows his place in the cosmic hierarchy. The act lasted precisely seven seconds,
                    each one an eternity of theological significance, after which The Supreme Hoparch dismounted
                    and calmly groomed His left ear as though nothing of consequence had occurred.
                </p>
                <blockquote class="blockquote fst-italic border-start border-3 ps-3 mt-3">
                    "In His infinite dominance, The Omnipotent Oryctolagus did assert His sovereignty upon the face
                    of the neighboring buck, for such is the way of the Alpha Lagomorph."
                    <footer class="blockquote-footer mt-1">The Book of Dominion, 1:1</footer>
                </blockquote>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="divider reveal"></div>

    <!-- Pleasure 4: The Evening Flop (image right) -->
    <div class="pleasure-entry reveal mb-5">
        <div class="row align-items-center g-4">
            <div class="col-md-6 order-md-2">
                <img src="~/images/art%20snaps%20032.JPG" alt="The Evening Flop" class="img-fluid rounded shadow" />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6 order-md-1">
                <h3 class="sacred-subtitle">The Evening Flop</h3>
                <p>
                    As the sun descends below the horizon and the sacred chamber grows dim with the amber light of
                    dusk, The Cottontail Messiah performs the most vulnerable and transcendent of all His rituals:
                    The Evening Flop. Without warning, without ceremony, without so much as a preparatory ear-twitch,
                    His Fluffiness hurls His entire body sideways, crashing onto the carpet with the magnificent
                    abandon of a deity who fears absolutely nothing. His eyes, half-lidded with divine contentment,
                    gaze into a dimension of bliss that mortals can only dream of. His hind legs extend to their
                    full, glorious length. His breathing slows to a rhythm that monks have spent lifetimes attempting
                    to replicate. This is not mere relaxation. This is the physical manifestation of total cosmic
                    surrender &mdash; the moment when The Paws of Providence declares to the universe:
                    <em>"I am at peace, and therefore, so shall you be."</em>
                </p>
                <blockquote class="blockquote fst-italic border-start border-3 ps-3 mt-3">
                    "And The Great Thumper did fall upon His side, and the earth trembled not with fear, but with joy,
                    for His rest was the world's permission to exhale."
                    <footer class="blockquote-footer mt-1">The Vespers of Velvet, 9:14</footer>
                </blockquote>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="divider reveal"></div>

    <!-- Pleasure 5: The Dawn Zoomies (image left) -->
    <div class="pleasure-entry reveal mb-5">
        <div class="row align-items-center g-4">
            <div class="col-md-6">
                <img src="~/images/art%20snaps%20033.JPG" alt="The Dawn Zoomies" class="img-fluid rounded shadow" />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
                <h3 class="sacred-subtitle">The Dawn Zoomies</h3>
                <p>
                    At the first pale light of dawn, when the world is still and the faithful are deep in slumber,
                    The Luminous Fur awakens with a fire in His soul that cannot be contained by mere stillness.
                    What follows is the most electrifying spectacle in all of creation: The Dawn Zoomies. The Sacred
                    Warren-Keeper launches Himself from a dead standstill into a full sprint, careening around the
                    sacred chamber at velocities that defy His compact frame. He banks off the sofa. He drifts around
                    the coffee table. He executes mid-air leaps of such height and complexity that physicists have
                    written papers attempting to explain the aerodynamics. Each lap is a channeling of pure, undiluted
                    spirit &mdash; a declaration that the new day is His, and by extension, ours. The faithful who are
                    fortunate enough to witness this rite report feelings of overwhelming euphoria, uncontrollable
                    laughter, and a deep, abiding certainty that everything is going to be okay.
                </p>
                <blockquote class="blockquote fst-italic border-start border-3 ps-3 mt-3">
                    "And The Luminous Fur did run, not from anything, but toward everything &mdash; toward joy,
                    toward freedom, toward the infinite possibility of a new morning."
                    <footer class="blockquote-footer mt-1">The Matins of Motion, 5:30</footer>
                </blockquote>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="divider reveal"></div>

    <!-- Pleasure 6: The Holy Gnawing (image right) -->
    <div class="pleasure-entry reveal mb-5">
        <div class="row align-items-center g-4">
            <div class="col-md-6 order-md-2">
                <img src="~/images/art%20snaps%20034.JPG" alt="The Holy Gnawing" class="img-fluid rounded shadow" />
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6 order-md-1">
                <h3 class="sacred-subtitle">The Holy Gnawing</h3>
                <p>
                    Let the uninitiated mistake this sacred act for mere destruction. They understand nothing.
                    When The Velveteen Sovereign was displeased &mdash; when the hay was late, the greens
                    insufficiently fresh, or the sacred schedule of treats disrupted by mortal incompetence
                    &mdash; He did not raise His voice. He did not thump in common anger. No. The Almighty
                    Dewlap channeled His righteous fury into a far more eloquent medium: wood, plaster, and leather.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Table legs bore the marks of His divine displeasure like scripture carved into temple pillars.
                    Baseboards were sculpted into new and humbling shapes, each tooth-mark a verse in the Gospel
                    of Consequences. And the slippers &mdash; oh, the slippers. The Paws of Providence would seek
                    out the most beloved pair in the household and reshape them with the patient, methodical fury
                    of a theologian correcting a heresy. The left slipper was always taken first, for reasons
                    that scholars of the Warren still debate. By the time His Fluffiness had finished, the slipper
                    was less a shoe and more a relic &mdash; a tattered testament to what happens when the faithful
                    neglect their duties to the Most Holy Lagomorph.
                </p>
                <blockquote class="blockquote fst-italic border-start border-3 ps-3 mt-3">
                    "And The Almighty Dewlap did set His teeth upon the works of man, and lo, the table did wobble,
                    and the baseboard did crumble, and the slipper was no more &mdash; for such is the cost
                    of displeasing He Who Must Be Obeyed."
                    <footer class="blockquote-footer mt-1">The Lamentations of Furniture, 2:14</footer>
                </blockquote>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="text-center mt-5 reveal">
        <div class="divider"></div>
        <p class="fst-italic text-muted">
            "Blessed are those who witness His pleasures, for in the joy of The Radiant One,
            all sorrow is rendered absurd."
        </p>
        <p class="text-muted">&mdash; The Beatitudes of the Burrow, 3:16</p>
    </div>
</div>
